{
  "requirement": "A `{req}` opening fence MAY contain four or more colons. Habeas MUST accept opening fences of any length of at least three colons. A colon-only line with fewer colons than the opener MUST remain inside the requirement body, allowing a nested colon directive. A closing fence outside a fenced code example with at least as many colons as the opener MUST end the body.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the implementation covers every obligation in `requirements.ci.fence_lengths`, and the tests cover the main cases. I found no unmarked implementation or unspecified behavior.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `colon_fence` (`src/lib.rs`, marked `{impl}`) accepts any line with at least three colons and returns the colon count. The opener therefore has no upper length limit, and any length of three or more is accepted.\n- `definition_blocks` (`src/lib.rs`, marked `{impl}`) records the opener's `fence_length` and recognizes `{req}`, `{example}` and `{definition}` after it.\n- It searches for the closer only among lines that:\n  - are not inside a Markdown code block (`overlaps_protected` against pulldown-cmark `CodeBlock` ranges);\n  - parse as a colon fence with `count >= fence_length`;\n  - have an empty suffix.\n- A colon-only line with fewer colons than the opener (for example `:::` inside a `::::` block) is never a closer, so it stays in the body. A nested `:::{note}` also has a non-empty suffix, so it stays in the body too.\n- A `:::` line inside a fenced code example is skipped as a closer. This is the \"outside a fenced code example\" clause.\n- Other consumers all go through `definition_blocks`: evidence validation (`src/evidence.rs`:283), mdbook preprocessing (`src/lib.rs`:161 and 262) and the term handling (`src/terms.rs`:88). The behavior is therefore consistent everywhere.\n- `src/index.rs` `marker_ranges` uses the same closer rule for `{requirements-index}`. That is a different directive, so it does not need this requirement's `{impl}` marker.\n\n**Tests**\n- `a_longer_colon_fence_contains_a_shorter_nested_directive` (`src/lib.rs`) covers a `::::` opener with a nested `:::{note}` and a `:::` line. It checks that the block is closed, that the body keeps the nested directive, and that the text after the closer is excluded.\n- `validates_four_or_more_colon_fences_and_longer_closers` (`src/evidence.rs`) runs end to end through `validate_repository` with these opener/closer pairs: `::::`/`::::`, `::::`/`:::::`, and `:::::::`/`::::::::`. The body contains a nested `:::{note}` block and trailing text.\n- `colon_requirement_span_includes_markdown_and_excludes_following_text` (`src/evidence.rs`) covers the ordinary three-colon opener. A `:::` line inside a fenced code example does not end the body. The body continues past headings and the text after the closer is excluded.\n\n**Gaps**\n- There is no explicit test for a closer shorter than the opener leaving the block unclosed. The logic that rejects it is the same `count >= fence_length` comparison that the nested-directive test exercises.\n- That is a minor omission and I do not treat it as a gap.",
